      <div class="article-copy">
        <div class="opening">
          <p>Have you seen Star Wars? Pretty cool, right? This is not that. This is a real problem that is likely imminent, and the funnest solution happens to be satellites that shoot lasers. Still pretty cool.</p>

          <p>An immense amount of hardware is going into orbit in the next decade: giant telecom networks, telescopes, data centres. Space is big, and avoiding things is easy while there are a few thousand of them. Applications now on file with regulators ask for about 1.3 million more satellites. Everything in low orbit moves at roughly 27,300 km/h. Put a hundred thousand objects in a Walker shell where the median gap between neighbours is 36 km, and collision avoidance stops being an occasional maneuver and becomes the main thing your satellites do. Starlink already reports 200,000 avoidance maneuvers every six months.</p>

          <p>Enter Kessler syndrome. A collision does not immediately produce the Sandra Bullock nightmare in <em>Gravity</em>. But it does produces a few thousand fragments in the same orbital band, each of which can hit something else, and a band where fragments are created faster than the atmosphere removes them, something that should still cause heavy breathing (for investors at least). ESA's 2026 environment report says low orbit is already there: even with no further launches, collisions among existing objects will grow the debris population. What changes with a million new satellites is the speed. The result is tens of thousands of pieces of expensive critical infrastructure getting destroyed, delayed exploration, and an expensive nightmare cleanup scenario.</p>

          <p>We are going to need attack satellites. They are just going to attack junk.</p>
        </div>

        <section id="what-is-up-there-now">
          <h2>1. What is up there now</h2>

