          <p>First, the dangerous mass is old: of the "top 50" derelicts experts most want removed, 34 are Soviet or Russian, 44 are rocket bodies, and 76% predate 2000; the worst are 9-tonne SL-16 stages at 700–900 km (<a href="https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2025/10/25/the-top-50-space-debris-items-that-should-be-removed-a-critical-look-at-orbital-threats/">McKnight et al., 2025 update</a>). ESA attributes 96% of assessed LEO risk to inactive objects. Second, the population is not stable. ESA's health index for the LEO environment went from about 4 to about 50 in a single year, and the agency shortened its modelling horizon from 200 years to 100 because the projected object count runs away.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="what-is-applied-for">
          <h2>2. What is applied for</h2>

          <p>These are requests and grants, not launches. The FCC took three years to partially grant SpaceX's previous 30,000-satellite application.</p>

